recipe plugin for DokuWiki

Mark a page's ingredient list with the <recipe> wrapper. The plugin then turns
the WHOLE page into an interactive, rescalable recipe:

  * the marked ingredient list is authoritative - every amount is converted to
    metric (g/kg, ml/l) and its ingredient NAME is learned into a per-page
    dictionary;
  * the rest of the page (the method, notes, ...) is scanned and anything that
    is either a number + a cooking unit ("half a cup of butter") or a number +
    a known ingredient ("beat in one egg") is rescaled too;
  * a servings control lets the reader change the yield and every amount on the
    page recalculates at once.

Numbers that are neither a unit nor a known ingredient are left alone, so
"bake for 20 minutes", "preheat to 350 degrees" and "a 9 inch pan" are never
touched.

Syntax
------

  <recipe 4>
    * 2 cups flour
    * 3 eggs
    * 3 oz butter, melted
    * 2 onions, finely chopped
    * salt to taste
  </recipe>

  ==== Method ====

  Whisk the flour and beat in one egg. Stir in half a cup of the butter,
  fold through two onions and bake for 20 minutes at 350 degrees.

The number after "recipe" is how many servings the listed amounts produce.
An optional title can be given: <recipe 12 title="Grandma's Cookies">.

Amounts may be written as integers, decimals (2.5 / 2,5), fractions (1/2),
mixed numbers (1 1/2), unicode fractions (1½), spelled-out words (half, a,
two, ...) or ranges (2-3 cups). Ingredient lines without a recognisable amount
("salt to taste") are left as-is but still contribute their name to the
dictionary.

How the dictionary works
------------------------

Because you listed "3 eggs" and "2 onions", the plugin knows that "egg" and
"onion" are ingredients in THIS recipe. That is what makes it safe to scale a
bare count like "one egg" in the method text while leaving "20 minutes"
untouched - it only scales unit-less numbers when the following word is an
ingredient you actually named.

Everything happens client-side with plain DOM manipulation, so pages stay
cacheable and the wiki server does no per-request maths. With JavaScript
disabled the reader simply sees the original, server-rendered page.

Configuration
-------------

  defaultservings  servings assumed when the tag omits a number (default 1)
  showoriginal     show the original amount as a hover tooltip (default on)

